            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\n\nThis was already discussed in #1840 which resulted into a [more complete `MiniSnippets.gen_loader.from_lang()` help snippet](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/bc34736e5b1d5561bc628f1cedd7c01f5ce5ee9f/doc/mini-snippets.txt#L825-L837).\n\nTL;DR:\n- With enabled tree-sitter in markdown files (which I assume is the case here) the 'mini.snippets' context can contain 'markdown' **or** 'markdown_inline' as a language. Since 'friendly-snippets' only contains snippets for [\"markdown\" language](https://github.com/rafamadriz/friendly-snippets/blob/main/snippets/markdown.json), setup for 'mini.snippets' has to be adjusted to also trigger those snippets for the \"markdown_inline\" language.\n- Using a local tree-sitter language is to be able to use dedicated snippets inside injected languages (like markdown code blocks, funnily enough). It also doesn't fall back to parent language (which for the case of \"markdown_inline\" is \"markdown\") because I find it confusing if markdown snippets are matched inside code block for language that doesn't have snippets.\n- Using the following setup works as expected (notice also one less nesting level for `MiniSnippets.start_lsp_server` argument):\n\n    ```lua\n    -- Adjust language patterns\n    local latex_patterns = { 'latex/**/*.json', '**/latex.json' }\n    local lang_patterns = {\n      tex = latex_patterns,\n      plaintex = latex_patterns,\n      -- Recognize special injected language of markdown tree-sitter parser\n      markdown_inline = { 'markdown.json' },\n    }\n    local gen_loader = require('mini.snippets').gen_loader\n    require('mini.snippets').setup({\n      snippets = {\n        gen_loader.from_file('~/.config/nvim/snippets/global.json'),\n        gen_loader.from_lang({ lang_patterns = lang_patterns }),\n      },\n    })\n    MiniSnippets.start_lsp_server({ match = false })\n    ```",
